00:17:28,622 --> 00:17:31,812
So if you're in your 30s, you use it as a Google search replacement.

181
00:17:32,046 --> 00:17:35,424
But if you're in your teens, ChatGPT is your gateway to the internet.

182
00:17:35,886 --> 00:17:38,064
What is the parallel here in coding today?

183
00:17:38,224 --> 00:17:49,860
If we were to watch two people code using OpenClaw, Cloud Code, Codecs, one you'd consider mediocre at it, and one you would consider fully AI native, how would you describe the difference?

184
00:17:51,822 --> 00:18:00,164
I mean, I think it's just trying to get the most out of the tools that are available, utilizing all of their features, investing into your own kind of setup.

185
00:18:00,704 --> 00:18:09,904
So just like previously, all the engineers are used to basically getting the most out of the tools you use, either it's Vim or VS Code, or now it's uh you know, Cloth Code or Codex or so on.

186
00:18:10,144 --> 00:18:17,956
So just investing into your setup and utilizing a lot of the tools that are available to you.

187
00:18:19,406 --> 00:18:21,104
and I think it just kind of looks like that.

188
00:18:21,264 --> 00:18:29,744
I do think that maybe related thought is a lot of people are maybe hiring for this, right?

189
00:18:29,824 --> 00:18:32,420
Because they want to hire strong agentic engineers.

190
00:18:32,704 --> 00:18:43,504
I do think that what I'm seeing is that most people have still not refactored their um their hiring process for agentic engineer capability, right?

191
00:18:43,790 --> 00:18:47,396
Like if you're giving out puzzles to solve, then this is still the old paradigm.

192
00:18:47,694 --> 00:18:53,344
I would say that hiring has to look like give me a really big project and see someone implement that big project.

193
00:18:53,424 --> 00:19:04,196
Like let's write, say, a Twitter clone for agents and then make it really good, make it really secure, and then have some agents simulate some activity on this Twitter.

194
00:19:04,622 --> 00:19:09,264
And then I'm gonna use 10 codecs 5.4 x high to try to break your

195
00:19:12,334 --> 00:19:17,220
website that you deployed, and they're gonna try to basically break it, and they should not be able to break it.

196
00:19:17,582 --> 00:19:19,104
And so maybe it looks like that, right?

197
00:19:19,184 --> 00:19:28,484
And so, yeah, watching people in that setting and building bigger projects and utilizing the tooling is maybe what I would look at for the most part.

198
00:19:29,134 --> 00:19:33,892
And as agents do more, what human skill do you think becomes more valuable, not less?

199
00:19:35,104 --> 00:19:36,704
so yeah, it's a good question.

200
00:19:36,864 --> 00:19:41,824
I think well, right now the answer is that the agents are kind of like these intern entities, right?

201
00:19:41,984 --> 00:19:44,004
So it's remarkable.

202
00:19:45,454 --> 00:19:50,916
you basically still have to be in charge of the aesthetics, the judgment, the taste, and a little bit of oversight.

203
00:19:51,264 --> 00:20:04,624
maybe one of my favorite examples of like the the weirdness of agents is for menu gen. Uh you sign up with a Google account, but you purchase credits using a Stripe account, and both of them have email addresses.

204
00:20:04,814 --> 00:20:15,344
And my agent actually tried to basically like when you purchase credits, it assigned it using the email address from Stripe to the Google email address.

205
00:20:15,630 --> 00:20:19,424
Like there wasn't a persistent user ID that that uh for people.

206
00:20:19,758 --> 00:20:26,436
It was trying to match up the email addresses, but you could use different email address for your Stripe and your Google, and basically would not associate the funds.

207
00:20:26,862 --> 00:20:30,064
And so this is the kind of thing that these agents still will make mistakes about.

208
00:20:30,254 --> 00:20:33,220
it's like, why would you use email addresses to try to cross-correlate the funds?

209
00:20:33,454 --> 00:20:34,224
They can be arbitrary.

210
00:20:34,304 --> 00:20:36,068
You can use different emails, et cetera.

211
00:20:36,174 --> 00:20:37,984
Like this is such a weird thing to do.

212
00:20:38,144 --> 00:20:41,572
So I think people have to be in charge of this spec, this plan.

213
00:20:41,984 --> 00:20:44,624
And I actually don't even like the plan mode.

214
00:20:44,704 --> 00:20:56,304
I I would, I mean, obviously it's very useful, but I think there's something more general here where you have to work with your agent to design a spec that is very detailed, and maybe it's basically the docs, and then get the agents to write them.

215
00:20:56,430 --> 00:21:01,476
And you're in charge of the oversight and the top-level categories, but the agents are doing a lot of the under the hood.

216
00:21:01,984 --> 00:21:04,676
And so I think you're not caring about some of the details.

217
00:21:04,910 --> 00:21:16,292
So, as an example, also with arrays or tensors in neural networks, there's a ton of details between PyTorch and NumPy and all the different like pandas and so on for all the different little API details.

218
00:21:16,544 --> 00:21:22,624
And I I already forgot about the keep dims versus keep dim, or whether it's dim or axis or reshape or permute or transpose.

219
00:21:22,784 --> 00:21:24,132
I don't remember this stuff anymore, right?

220
00:21:24,304 --> 00:21:25,024
Because you don't have to.

221
00:21:25,262 --> 00:21:28,612
This is the kind of details that are handled by the intern because they have very good recall.

222
00:21:29,104 --> 00:21:39,344
but you still have to know, for example, that um, you know, there's underlying tensor, there's an underlying view, and then you can manipulate view of the same storage, or you can have different storage, which would be less efficient.

223
00:21:39,630 --> 00:21:47,940
And so you still have to have an understanding of what this stuff is doing and some of the fundamentals, so that you're not copying memory around unnecessarily and so on.

224
00:21:48,384 --> 00:21:51,460
But the details of the APIs are not handed off.

225
00:21:51,918 --> 00:22:02,148
So you're in charge of the taste, the engineering, the design, and that it makes sense, and that you're asking for the right things, and that you're saying that okay, these have to be unique user IDs that we're going to tie everything to.

226
00:22:03,630 --> 00:22:08,164
and so you're doing some of the Design and development and the engineers are doing the fill in the blanks.

227
00:22:08,558 --> 00:22:09,920
And that's currently kind of like where we are.

228
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:13,200
And I think that's what everyone, of course, is seeing, I think, right now.

229
00:22:13,742 --> 00:22:20,612
Do you think there's a chance that this taste and judgment matters less over time, or will the ceiling just keep rising?

230
00:22:21,360 --> 00:22:22,320
yeah, it's a good question.

231
00:22:22,480 --> 00:22:23,204
I would say

232
00:22:26,766 --> 00:22:28,640
I mean, I'm hoping that it improves.

233
00:22:28,720 --> 00:22:32,480
I think probably the reason it doesn't improve right now is again, it's not part of the RL.

234
00:22:32,560 --> 00:22:37,828
There's probably no aesthetics cost or reward, or it's not good enough, or something like that.

235
00:22:39,950 --> 00:22:52,772
I do think that when you actually look at the code, sometimes I get a little bit of a heart attack because it's not like super amazing code necessarily all the time, and it's very bloaty, and there's a lot of copy paste, and there's awkward abstractions that are brittle and like it works, but it's just really gross.

236
00:22:54,126 --> 00:22:56,996
and I do I do hope that this can improve in future models.

237
00:22:57,582 --> 00:23:05,120
a good example also is this uh you know the micro GPT project, uh, which where I was trying to simplify LLM training to be as simple as possible.

238
00:23:05,326 --> 00:23:07,140
The models hate this, they can't do it.

239
00:23:07,360 --> 00:23:12,484
I tried to, I keep, I kept trying to prompt an LLM to simplify more, simplify more, and it just can't.

240
00:23:13,294 --> 00:23:15,332
You feel like you're outside of the RL circuits.

241
00:23:15,520 --> 00:23:19,044
It feels like you're obviously, you know, you're pulling teeth.

242
00:23:19,200 --> 00:23:20,900
It's not like light speed.

243
00:23:21,742 --> 00:23:27,840
So I think um, I do think that people still remain in charge of this, but I do think that there's nothing fundamental again that's preventing it.

244
00:23:28,020 --> 00:23:29,540
It's just the labs haven't done it yet almost.

245
00:23:30,414 --> 00:23:30,640
Yeah.

246
00:23:31,214 --> 00:23:36,000
So I'd love to come back to this idea of jagged forms of intelligence.

247
00:23:36,160 --> 00:23:41,040
You wrote a little bit about this with a very thought-provoking piece around animals versus ghosts.

248
00:23:41,680 --> 00:23:45,680
and the idea is that we're not building animals, we are summoning ghosts.

249
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:55,684
and these are jagged forms of intelligence that are shaped by data and reward functions, but not by intrinsic motivation or fun or curiosity or empowerment.

250
00:23:56,718 --> 00:23:59,172
things that kind of came about via evolution.

251
00:24:00,398 --> 00:24:01,700
why does that framing matter?

252
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:07,684
And what does it actually change about how you build and deploy and evaluate or even trust them?

253
00:24:09,518 --> 00:24:15,600
yeah, so yeah, I think the reason I wrote about this is because I'm trying to wrap my head around what these things are, right?

254
00:24:15,790 --> 00:24:20,880
Because if you have a good model of what they are or are not, then you're going to be more competent at using them.

255
00:24:21,710 --> 00:24:28,000
and I do think that I don't know if it has, I'm not sure if it actually has like real power.

256
00:24:28,400 --> 00:24:30,436
I think it's a little bit of philosophizing.

257
00:24:31,790 --> 00:24:43,812
but I do think that I think it's just coming to terms with the fact that these things are not you know animal intelligences, like if you yell at them, they're not gonna work better or worse, or it doesn't have any impact.

258
00:24:44,974 --> 00:24:53,700
and it's all just kind of like these statistical simulation circuits where the the substrate is pre-training, so like statistics.

259
00:24:54,254 --> 00:24:59,044
And then but then there's RL bolting on top, so it kind of like increases the dispendages.

260
00:24:59,440 --> 00:25:06,000
And maybe it's just kind of like a mindset of what I'm coming into or what's likely to work or not likely to work or how to modify it.

261
00:25:06,350 --> 00:25:11,760
But I don't actually I don't know that I have like here are the five obvious outcomes of how to make your system better.

262
00:25:11,950 --> 00:25:15,652
It's more just being suspicious of it and figuring out over time.

263
00:25:16,494 --> 00:25:17,156
That's where it starts.

264
00:25:17,680 --> 00:25:20,516
okay, so you are so deep in working with agents that don't just chat.

265
00:25:20,960 --> 00:25:26,916
They have real permissions, they have local context, they actually take action on your behalf.

266
00:25:27,246 --> 00:25:30,468
What does the world look like when we all start to live in that world?

267
00:25:31,440 --> 00:25:39,840
yeah, I think a lot of people probably here are excited about what this agentic uh you know native agentic environment looks like.

268
00:25:39,920 --> 00:25:44,996
And everything has to be rewritten, everything is still fundamentally written for humans and has to be moved around.

269
00:25:45,422 --> 00:25:52,000
I still use most of the time when I use different frameworks or libraries or things like that, they still have docs that are fundamentally written for humans.

270
00:25:52,080 --> 00:25:53,636
This is my favorite pet peeve.

271
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,720
Like, I don't why are people still telling me what to do?

272
00:25:56,814 --> 00:25:57,760
Like, I don't want to do anything.

273
00:25:57,998 --> 00:26:00,080
What is the thing I should copy paste to my agent?

274
00:26:00,320 --> 00:26:07,652
Like so it's just every time I'm told, you know, go to this URL or something like that, it's just like, oh you know.

275
00:26:08,398 --> 00:26:19,120
So everyone is, I think, excited about how do we decompose the workloads that need to happen into fundamentally sensors over the world, actuators over the world, how do we make it agent native?

276
00:26:19,360 --> 00:26:31,268
basically describe it to agents first, and then have a lot of automation around um, you know, the um yeah, around uh data structures that are very legible to the LLMs.

277
00:26:32,080 --> 00:26:39,040
So I think, yeah, I'm hoping that there's a lot of agent first infrastructure out there and that, you know, for Menu Gen, famously.

278
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:41,280
when I wrote the —I'm, not sure how famously.

279
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:50,880
but when I wrote the blog post about Menu Gen, um, a lot of the work, a lot of the trouble was not even writing the code for Menu Gen. it was deploying it in Vercel because I had to work with all these different services.

280
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:52,960
and I had to string them up and I had to go to their settings.

281
00:26:52,960 --> 00:26:55,904
and the menus and, you know, configure my DNS.

282
00:26:55,984 --> 00:26:57,156
and it was just so annoying.

283
00:26:57,424 --> 00:27:08,224
And so that's a good example of I would hope that MenuGen that I could give a prompt to an LLM build menu gen and that I didn't have to touch anything and it's deployed in that same way on the internet.

284
00:27:08,704 --> 00:27:14,436
I think that would be a good kind of uh test for whether or not a lot of our infrastructure is becoming more and more agent native.

285
00:27:14,862 --> 00:27:22,788
And then ultimately I would say, yeah, I do think we're going towards a world where there's agent representation for people and for organizations.

286
00:27:23,424 --> 00:27:31,184
And um, you know, I'll have my agent talk to your agent to figure out some of the details of our meetings or things like that.

287
00:27:31,406 --> 00:27:35,584
So um I do think that that's roughly where things are going.

288
00:27:35,854 --> 00:27:37,904
But yeah, I think everyone here is excited about that.

289
00:27:38,224 --> 00:27:40,944
I really like the visual analogy of sensors and actuators.

290
00:27:41,024 --> 00:27:41,984
I actually hadn't thought of that.

291
00:27:42,064 --> 00:27:42,784
That's super interesting.

292
00:27:43,854 --> 00:27:56,144
OK, I think we have to end on a question about education, because you are probably one of the very best in the world at making complex technical concepts simple and deeply thoughtful about how we design education around it.

293
00:27:57,262 --> 00:28:04,836
what still remains worth learning deeply when intelligence gets cheap as we move into the next era of AI?

294
00:28:05,390 --> 00:28:05,700
Yeah.

295
00:28:06,384 --> 00:28:10,064
there was a tweet that blew my mind recently, and I keep thinking about it like every other day.

296
00:28:10,318 --> 00:28:15,364
It was something along the lines of you can outsource your thinking, but you can't outsource your understanding.

297
00:28:16,558 --> 00:28:17,384
And um

298
00:28:17,384 --> 00:28:18,692
I think that's really nicely put.

299
00:28:19,504 --> 00:28:27,104
I so, yeah, because I still I'm still part of the system, and I still I still have to somehow information still has to make it into my brain.

300
00:28:27,184 --> 00:28:34,864
And I feel like I'm becoming a bottleneck of just even knowing what are we trying to build, why is it worth doing, how do I direct, my agents and so on.

301
00:28:35,566 --> 00:28:41,092
So I do still think that ultimately something has to direct the thinking and the processing and so on.

302
00:28:41,504 --> 00:28:45,572
And that's still kind of fundamentally constrained somehow by understanding.

303
00:28:45,710 --> 00:28:52,144
And this is one reason I also was very excited about all the LLM knowledge bases, because I feel like that's a way for me to process information.

304
00:28:52,366 --> 00:28:56,388
And anytime I see a different projection onto information, I always feel like I gain insight.

305
00:28:56,750 --> 00:29:01,700
So it's really just a lot of prompts for me to do synthetic data generation kind of over some fixed data.

306
00:29:02,384 --> 00:29:16,580
so I really enjoy whenever I read an article, I have my wiki that's being built up from these articles, and I love asking questions about things or um, and I think that ultimately these are tools to enhance understanding in a certain way.

307
00:29:16,910 --> 00:29:26,304
And this is still kind of like a bit of a bottleneck because then you can't direct the you can't be a good director if you still because the L on certainly don't excel at understanding.

308
00:29:26,384 --> 00:29:27,940
You still are uniquely in charge of that.

309
00:29:28,718 --> 00:29:32,784
So yeah, I think tools to that effect, I think, are incredibly interesting and exciting.

310
00:29:33,294 --> 00:29:40,144
I'm excited to be back here in a couple years and to see if we've been fully automated out of the loop and they actually take care of understanding as well.

311
00:29:40,544 --> 00:29:41,984
thank you so much for joining us, Andre.

312
00:29:42,144 --> 00:29:43,264
I really appreciate it.

